            <div xml:id="d1192">

                <pb facs="1243" n="1231" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg1231"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.67/4–745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-04-07T19:00:00+02:00">April 7, 1945—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 7—4:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3553. An official of the Foreign Office today said that British representatives
                    in Bulgaria had become somewhat alarmed at stories current in Sofia that the
                    Russians contemplated some sort of aggressive action in Thrace against Turkey.
                    The Foreign Office, however, is of the opinion that these stories have been
                    “planted”, probably by the Russians in their current war of nerves against
                    Turkey. The official stated that it was fanciful even to consider that Russia
                    would launch an act of aggression against Turkey at the present juncture
                    “although this might not be the case in 2 or 3 years”.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Caserta, Moscow, Ankara.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
